This, I don't understand.  I think you are letting the perceived source
of this mechanic to cloud your judgement.  Personally, I thought the
Monk "Activate by Movement" was a very clever game tactics.
Traditionally, it is hard to give abilities to melee fighters.  Having
melee fighters select abilities from menus seems too much like what
wizards do.  Activate by Movement has the advantage of letting melee
fighters do what they do best - mash the direction arrows.   It
provides a very different tactical feel to setting off the ability.
The layout of the map - always the strongest point in roguelikes -
becomes essential.  Activating the ability isn't just a matter of
having enough Power saved up - you must have enough room to execute the
ability.  For example, a Charge skill could be activated whenever one
attacks in the direction one moved last turn.  No need for the player
to choose Charge from a menu, or ensure the cost in Power for charging
is high enough.  The player charges by actually charging and the cost
is getting oneself in a position where you will be able to move in the
direction you are going to attack next.  Or, as POWDER already has, a
Moving Target skill might grant a dodge bonus provided one moved the
previous turn.

Indeed, IIRC, don't cloaks of displacement already act as an Activate
by Movement?

Move to Activate is a feature that is on my to-be-stolen list for
POWDER.
